From be2bd9405ce2c1a4ea93d5ca57a1d25195956e0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:34:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] scene: Draw the background below blending windows in each eye
 of a stereo frame

A 2D window whose surface says it is opaque where its pixels carry alpha
showed another picture in the right eye: in a stereo capture of ORDER
296's probe, (204, 51, 51) in 2D and in the left eye, (255, 76, 76) in
the right eye, over exactly the 60,000 pixels at alpha one half.

Occlusion culling in paintSimpleScreen() leaves what lies below an
opaque region undrawn, the background included, and the item renderer
blends a surface with an alpha channel over whatever the target holds
there. The wayland.xml text of wl_surface.set_opaque_region allows this
("marking transparent content as opaque will result in repaint
artifacts"), but in a stereo frame the target holds another picture
there: renderStereoImage() in the screenshot plugin draws the right eye
into the target that holds the left eye's, and on a 3D output each eye
is drawn into buffers that hold its own earlier frames, with damage
journals of their own. So the right eye of a capture showed the window
blended over the left eye's picture of it, and the two eyes of a 3D
output could each show another accumulation.

In each eye of a stereo frame the background is now drawn below every
window whose content blends, as a 2D capture has it: both eyes show such
a surface over the same transparent black, whatever the target held.
What lies below a window that covers it is still not drawn. The part of
the opaque region that surfaces without an alpha channel draw without
blending, at full opacity and not cut by a rounded corner, keeps the
whole saving, because they replace whatever the target holds. 2D frames
are drawn as before.

Tests: testScreenshotStereo's testTranslucentPixelsUnderOpaqueRegion
(the right eye of a capture, with a declared window on a 2D output and
on a 3D output) and testEyePassOverEarlierPicture (an eye pass into a
target that holds another picture) fail without this change, with 800
of the 800 translucent pixels showing another colour.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
